<div class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Does AI improve learning?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">It depends on how the tool is designed. A Harvard randomized trial found a carefully built AI tutor beat an active-learning lesson on one short physics topic, while a PNAS field experiment found students who practised with an unguarded chatbot did worse once it was removed. The evidence does not support a general claim that AI improves learning.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Does AI save teachers time?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Teachers say it does. In a Gallup survey of 2,232 US public school teachers, those who use AI at least weekly estimated they save 5.9 hours a week. These are self-reported estimates and the survey did not measure student learning.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Can students be harmed by AI tools?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Yes, if the tool simply gives answers. In the PNAS study, students who practised with a standard chatbot interface scored 17% lower on a later test without it than students who never had access. A version with learning guardrails largely avoided that harm.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Is there evidence from Nepal?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">We did not find a trial or teacher survey from Nepal in the sources used here. The studies come from the United States, Turkey and Nigeria, so any school in Nepal should treat them as a guide for a small local pilot, not as proof of local results.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

